NNAAMMEE
       sigreturn,  rt_sigreturn - return from signal handler and cleanup stack
       frame

LLIIBBRRAARRYY
       Standard C library (_l_i_b_c, _-_l_c)

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       iinntt ssiiggrreettuurrnn((......));;

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       If the Linux kernel determines that an unblocked signal is pending  for
       a  process,  then,  at  the  next  transition back to user mode in that
       process (e.g., upon return from a system call or when  the  process  is
       rescheduled  onto  the  CPU),  it creates a new frame on the user‐space
       stack where it saves various pieces of process context (processor  sta‐
       tus word, registers, signal mask, and signal stack settings).

       The kernel also arranges that, during the transition back to user mode,
       the  signal  handler is called, and that, upon return from the handler,
       control passes to a piece of user‐space code commonly called the  "sig‐
       nal trampoline".  The signal trampoline code in turn calls ssiiggrreettuurrnn().

       This  ssiiggrreettuurrnn()  call  undoes  everything  that was done—changing the
       process’s signal mask, switching signal stacks (see  ssiiggaallttssttaacckk(2))—in
       order  to  invoke  the  signal handler.  Using the information that was
       earlier  saved  on  the  user‐space  stack  ssiiggrreettuurrnn()  restores   the
       process’s signal mask, switches stacks, and restores the process’s con‐
       text  (processor  flags  and registers, including the stack pointer and
       instruction pointer), so that the  process  resumes  execution  at  the
       point where it was interrupted by the signal.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       ssiiggrreettuurrnn() never returns.

VVEERRSSIIOONNSS
       Many  UNIX‐type  systems have a ssiiggrreettuurrnn() system call or near equiva‐
       lent.  However, this call is not specified in POSIX, and details of its
       behavior vary across systems.

SSTTAANNDDAARRDDSS
       None.

NNOOTTEESS
       ssiiggrreettuurrnn() exists only to allow the implementation of signal handlers.
       It should nneevveerr be called  directly.   (Indeed,  a  simple  ssiiggrreettuurrnn()
       wrapper  in  the  GNU  C  library  simply returns -1, with _e_r_r_n_o set to
       EENNOOSSYYSS.)  Details of the arguments (if any) passed to ssiiggrreettuurrnn()  vary
       depending on the architecture.  (On some architectures, such as x86‐64,
       ssiiggrreettuurrnn()  takes  no  arguments, since all of the information that it
       requires is available in the stack frame that was previously created by
       the kernel on the user‐space stack.)

       Once upon a time, UNIX systems placed the signal trampoline  code  onto
       the  user stack.  Nowadays, pages of the user stack are protected so as
       to disallow code execution.  Thus, on contemporary Linux  systems,  de‐
       pending on the architecture, the signal trampoline code lives either in
       the  vvddssoo(7)  or in the C library.  In the latter case, the C library’s
       ssiiggaaccttiioonn(2) wrapper function informs the kernel of the location of the
       trampoline code by placing its address in the _s_a___r_e_s_t_o_r_e_r field of  the
       _s_i_g_a_c_t_i_o_n  structure,  and  sets  the  SSAA__RREESSTTOORREERR flag in the _s_a___f_l_a_g_s
       field.

       The saved process context information is placed in a _u_c_o_n_t_e_x_t___t  struc‐
       ture (see _<_s_y_s_/_u_c_o_n_t_e_x_t_._h_>).  That structure is visible within the sig‐
       nal  handler  as the third argument of a handler established via ssiiggaacc‐‐
       ttiioonn(2) with the SSAA__SSIIGGIINNFFOO flag.

       On some other UNIX systems, the operation of the signal trampoline dif‐
       fers a little.  In particular, on some systems, upon transitioning back
       to user mode, the kernel passes control to the trampoline (rather  than
       the  signal  handler), and the trampoline code calls the signal handler
       (and then calls ssiiggrreettuurrnn() once the handler returns).

   CC lliibbrraarryy//kkeerrnneell ddiiffffeerreenncceess
       The original Linux system call was named  ssiiggrreettuurrnn().   However,  with
       the  addition  of  real‐time  signals  in Linux 2.2, a new system call,
       rrtt__ssiiggrreettuurrnn() was added to support an enlarged _s_i_g_s_e_t___t type.  The GNU
       C library hides these details from us, transparently  employing  rrtt__ssii‐‐
       ggrreettuurrnn() when the kernel provides it.

